The '''Wallche''' ({{IPAc-en|w|ɔː|l|ˈ|tʃ|i}} {{respell|wawl|CHEE|}}, {{IPA|ca|waʎˈtʃe|label=Walldungu and Volisanian:}}), also known as '''Tinguiriricans''', are a group of [[Indigenous peoples of Lurando-Abyala|Indigenous]] inhabitants of [[Cerdanya]], within the [[Northern Triangle]]. The collective term refers to a wide-ranging ethnicity composed of various groups who share a common social, religious, and economic structure, as well as a common linguistic heritage as [[Walldungu language|Walldungu]] speakers. Their homelands once extended from [[Llanquihue]] to [[Cap de Pingüins]] and later spread southward to [[Willi Mapu]], a land comprising part of the [[Tinguirirican Desert]] and the [[Bayaran savanna]]. Today, the Wallche represent 80.11% of Cerdanya's [[Indigenous peoples in Cerdanya|indigenous peoples]] and about 12% of the total community population. The Wallche population is concentrated in the mountainous interior. Many have migrated from [[Rural society in Balisca|rural areas]] to the cities of [[Merced]] and [[Concepción]] to pursue educational and economic opportunities.

== Etymology ==
Scholars believe that the various Wallche groups ([[Lafkenche]], [[Pewenche]], [[Puelche]], etc.) called themselves ''Reche'' during the early Volisaniuan colonial period, due to what they referred to as their pure native blood, derived from ''re'' meaning "pure" and ''che'' meaning "people".

However, Wallche is a relatively recent endonym meaning "the people that be" or "children that exist", with ''wall'' meaning "to be/exist"  and ''che'' meaning "person". It is preferred as a term when referring to the people after the [[Tinguiririca conflict]].

